 (cite5†v1 ), last revised 5 Sep 2026 (this version, v4)]\nL9: # Title:PAN: A World Model for General, Actionable, and Long-Horizon World Simulation\nL10: \nL11: Authors:cite6†PAN Team Institute of Foundation Models : cite7†Zihan Liu , cite8†Yi Gu , cite9†Mingkai Deng , cite10†Guangyi Liu , cite11†Zeyu Feng , cite12†Qiyue Gao , cite13†Yiyan Hu , cite14†Benhao Huang , cite15†Yichi Yang , cite16†Kun Zhou , cite17†Jiannan Xiang , cite18†Zhiting Hu , cite7†Zhengzhong Liu , cite19†Eric P. Xing L12: View a PDF of the paper titled PAN: A World Model for General, Actionable, and Long-Horizon World Simulation, by PAN Team Institute of Foundation Models: Zihan Liu and 13 other authors\nL13: \nL14: cite20†View PDF cite21†HTML (experimental) L15: > Abstract:A world model is a cognitive simulator of the real-world environment allowing biological agents to reason about how the world evolves, whether spontaneously or in response to their actions, and accordingly to plan and strategize.\nL16: In building Artificial Intelligence (AI) systems, world models represent the next frontier beyond large language models (LLMs) to enable physical and embodied intelligence in AI agents, allowing them to perform decision-making through simulative reasoning and reinforcement-learning through simulative trials.\nL17: Recent advancements in world modeling have yielded impressive progress in video generation, 3-D scene evolution, robotic dynamics, and game simulation, but limitations persist in general, open-domain, action-driven prediction, long-horizon consistency, and abstract reasoning and planning. Moreover, fundamental architectural questions, whether it be state representation, information flow, or training objectives, remain unresolved.\nL18: In this paper, we introduce PAN, a world model built on the Generative Latent Prediction (GLP) architecture. GLP combines stateful latent representations of world states; an encoder--decoder closed-loop information flow; an LLM/diffusion-based mixed reasoning backbone; and a non-degenerate generative reconstruction objective whose fidelity is ``dampable'' to balance fine-grained detail against semantic saliency.\nL19: Compared to several existing systems, PAN demonstrates advantages beyond standard video generation in action-conditioned world simulation, long-horizon forecasting, and simulative reasoning and planning, capabilities we argue should serve as the primary criteria for evaluating world models.\nL20: Subjects:  | Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV); Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI); Computation and Language (cs.CL); Machine Learning (cs.LG)\nL21: Cite as:  | cite22†arXiv:2511.09057 [cs.CV]\nL22:    | (or cite23†arXiv:2511.09057v4 [cs.CV] for this version)\nL23:    | cite24†https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2511.09057†doi.org arXiv-issued DOI via DataCite\nL24: ## Submission history\nL25: \nL26: From: Mingkai Deng [cite25†view email ]\nL27: cite5†[v1] Wed, 12 Nov 2025 07:20:35 UTC (34,352 KB)\nL28: cite26†[v2] Thu, 13 Nov 2025 02:28:41 UTC (34,352 KB)\nL29: cite27†[v3] Sat, 15 Nov 2025 01:07:10 UTC (34,352 KB)\nL30: [v4] Sat, 5 Sep 2026 22:08:15 UTC (9,269 KB)\nL31: \nL32: Full-text links:\nL33: ## Access Paper:\nL34: \nL35: View a PDF of the paper titled PAN: A World Model for General, Actionable, and Long-Horizon World Simulation, by PAN Team Institute of Foundation Models: Zihan Liu and 13 other authors\nL36: \nL37:   * cite20†View PDF L38:   * cite21†HTML (experimental) L39:   * cite28†TeX Source L40: \nL41: cite29†view license†creativecommons.org L42: \nL43: ### Current browse context:\nL44: \nL45: cs.CV\nL46: \nL47: cite30†< prev |   cite31†next > L48: \nL49: cite32†new | cite33†recent | cite34†2025-11 L50: \nL51: Change to browse by:\nL52: \nL53: cite35†cs L54: cite36†cs.AI L55: cite37†cs.CL L56: cite38†cs.LG L57: ### References & Citations\nL58: \nL59:   * cite39†NASA ADS†ui